raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Among American X/Twitter users, are the people who express political hostility also those who have the motivation, knowledge, and habits of participation usually associated with democratic citizenship?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We examine hostile expression in political exchanges on X/Twitter and ask whether\nsuch expression is associated with the same forms of engagement that are typically viewed as central to democratic\ncitizenship.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is the association between engagement and hostility stronger for users who feel more positively about their own party’s leaders than about the opposing party’s leaders?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Second, we provide suggestive analyses of whether these associations differ by affective polarization [22],\nmeasured as polarized affect towards political leaders.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Under this logic, the association between engagement and hostility should\nbe stronger among users who evaluate opposing political elites more negatively.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do more hostile users have more opportunities to be heard, as indicated by how much they post and the size of their networks?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 3. Online political hostility is associated with greater activity and potential reach on X/Twitter, measured by\nposting frequency and numbers of friends and followers.\nWe treat this final expectation as exploratory in nature given the lack of existing research.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Hostile political expression is not confined to people who are alienated from politics. Among the American X/Twitter users studied, it is positively associated with several of the same capacities, motivations, and activities valued in democratic life.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nfindings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based\nexplanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered\ncentral to democratic citizenship.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Who produces hostile posts may help explain their visibility: people who express more hostility have more opportunities to put their speech before an audience, apart from any attention the content itself attracts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This suggests that hostile political\nexpression may be more visible on X/Twitter not only because hostile content attracts attention, but also because users\nwho express such hostility are more active and have greater potential reach [3].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A practical implication is that changing platforms alone may be insufficient; efforts to reduce hostility may also need to address partisan animosity and the norms set by political leaders.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "However, if online political hostility is also associated with political engagement and out-party animus, platform-level interventions alone may be insufficient.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Reducing hostile political\nengagement online may also require attention to broader political polarization and the role of political elites in shaping\nnorms of partisan ‌‌conflict.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors recruited a US YouGov panel starting in January 2019, added panelists in September 2020, and fielded six survey waves through January 2021. They linked surveys to X accounts for 2,567 people; X-only analyses included up to 3,643.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All participants were recruited through YouGov’s standing web panel in the United States.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We recruited the original set of panelists in January 2019.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nparticipants, along with an additional set of panelists who were included to increase the study’s power in September 2020,\nwere then asked to participate in five additional survey waves, administered at various time points until the final survey in\nJanuary 2021.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period\nwas 2567. For the analyses where we only use X data the maximum number of respondents is 3643.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "With participants’ consent, the researchers collected more than nine million public posts between March 2019 and January 2021. The unit of analysis was the person, not the post.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants were specifically asked if they wished to share their X handle\nto link their publicly available posts to their survey responses.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The scraping of X commenced on March 4, 2019 and stopped in January 2021. During this period, we managed to\nscrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists. All measures based on X data are aggregated at the level of the individual\npanelist since our unit of analysis is individual panelists and not posts.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The surveys distinguished education and perceived political competence from interest, factual knowledge, and reported participation. The participation batteries covered eleven offline activities and ten online activities.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "From the surveys, we obtained individual-level measures of respondents’\neducation, sense of internal efficacy, political knowledge, political interest, self-reported participation in offline settings,\nand self-reported participation in online settings.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Political knowledge is measured using five factual questions from the ANES, scored as the number of correct\nanswers.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "To measure traditional offline participation, we created an 11-item battery\nbased on previous research [70,71], asking participants on scales ranging from “1 = Never” to “7 = Several times a day”\nhow often they participate in eleven offline political activities",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "To measure online participation, we asked participants on the same response scale how often\nthey engaged in ten online political activities [72,73]",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Leader-directed polarization was the difference between a respondent’s average warmth toward three leaders from their own party and three from the other party.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Respondents rated three Republican politicians (Ted Cruz, Donald Trump,\nand Mike Pence) and three Democratic politicians (Joe Biden, Elizabeth Warren, and Bernie Sanders) on a 0–100 feeling\nthermometer.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "We used respondents’ party identification to distinguish in-party from out-party leaders and calculated the\ndifference between average in-party and out-party leader ratings.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary outcome used word embeddings learned from panelists’ posts. Each post was scored by cosine similarity to a vector combining political and hostile language; scores were then averaged by user or by user and time period.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we create word vectors based on posts from our panelists using the word2vec method [61,62].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, we combine the word vectors\n“political” and “hate” into a combined vector that captures the co-occurrence of political and hostile language in the corpus.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Third, we use this combined measure to calculate the cosine similarity between individual posts and the “political hate”\nword vector:",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Fourth and finally, we create a\nmeasure of a respondent’s general level of political hostility by averaging the hostility scores of all their posts; for the panel\nmodels we compute this average per time unit investigated (wave or month, depending on the analysis).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper draws on several validations of the embedding score. In a comparison using expert-labeled posts, the score classified a held-out set with 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This\nmethod has been validated in [60], where its face validity, convergent and discriminant validity, criterion validity, external\nvalidity and ecological validity were established.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The SU approach was also validated against 2,469 hand-labeled posts rated by four expert raters",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The main cross-sectional comparisons used pooled regression with uncertainty clustered by user. They adjusted for broad personality, demographics, and partisanship, and addressed missing observations across waves.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the cross-sectional analyses, we use ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with cluster robust standard errors to\naccount for the fact that a single X user can participate across multiple waves. This dataset is organized as a Time-Series\nCross-Section (TSCS) dataset using pooled OLS.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Not all measures are available in each survey wave and we therefore\nuse multiple imputation and full information maximum likelihood to estimate missing values in these analyses.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for the Big\nFive personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Robustness tests compared the primary outcome with sentiment, toxicity, separate political and hostility components, and a supervised classifier. They also tested political relevance and adjusted for how much of each user’s output was political.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We estimate these associations using our primary measure of online political hostility and\nthe two secondary robustness measures, AFINN sentiment scores and Perspective API toxicity scores.",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Fig 2 provides a replication of the analyses above where the composite dependent variable of online political hostility is\nsubdivided into the two components of “political” and “hate” (i.e., general hostility). In addition, as described above, we\nalso use an alternative supervised classifier of online political hostility based on a sample of hand labelled posts.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "As a further robustness test we also created a model where we interact the Perspective API toxicity scores and AFINN\nscores with a political relevance score",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, we have also created a model where we control for the share of political posts among all posts posted by\neach user.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "To examine polarization, the authors compared users above and below the scale midpoint and estimated formal interaction terms. These models adjusted for age and gender rather than the full set of controls.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fig 4 displays the associations separately for users above and below the midpoint\nof the leader-directed affective-polarization scale.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Formal interaction coefficients between indicators of political engagement, its antecedents, and leader-directed affective polarization.\nAll models control for age and gender but not the full set of controls to reduce problems of multicollinearity.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random-intercept cross-lagged panel models used behavioral measures to distinguish changes within a person over time from stable differences between people.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM). These models separate\nstable between-person differences from within-person change",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "To examine whether the same pattern appears in\ntemporal within-person models, we use RI-CLPMs with behavioral measures of online political engagement and online\nhostility.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked for multiple testing separately within four families of analyses, using false-discovery-rate correction and Bonferroni-adjusted intervals.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define four separate families of tests: the primary association models,\nthe robustness outcome models, the affective-polarization interaction models, and the exploratory activity/reach models.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Within each family we apply a Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction and, for the more conservative\nreading requested by the reviewers, also report Bonferroni-adjusted 95% confidence intervals.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Exploratory negative-binomial models related hostility to three counts: posts, accounts followed, and followers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The\nexploratory activity and reach models are an exception: these are negative-binomial count models, and their estimates are\nreported on the original count scale.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "We investigate the visibility of politically hostile users by first showing results from count models that examine whether\nthose who are politically hostile (a) post more, (b) have more friends, and (c) have more followers. A “friend” in X’s usage\nis someone I follow, and a “follower” is someone who follows me.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A supplementary Danish X/Twitter sample provides a limited comparison: hostility there also correlated with political interest, efficacy, and news consumption, and less strongly with negative views of the political system.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Some tentative evidence of broader applicability is provided in S1 Appendix, where online political hostility in a sample of\nDanish X/Twitter users is also positively associated with users’ political interest, internal efficacy, and news consumption,\nand where the relationship extends – to a lesser extent – to users with negative views of the political system [14,50].",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "The clearest positive associations with hostile expression were for political interest, factual knowledge, and online participation. The overall pattern persisted after adjustment for broad personality traits, demographics, and party identification.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the\nstrongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The associations were obtained after controlling for\nbroad personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The relationship was not simply an artifact of engaged users writing about politics more often. It remained after adjusting for the political share of their posts, and alternative measures broadly supported it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The results from these analyses are presented in S1 Appendix and show that the main results are robust to this\nadditional control; if anything the results are slightly stronger when controlling for the share of political posts.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The robustness analyses are broadly consistent with the main pattern, with significant and sizeable associations across\nall three measures.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "More polarized feelings about party leaders were associated with a stronger participation–hostility link, especially for online and offline participation. This was not true uniformly across engagement indicators: only eight of eighteen interactions survived false-discovery-rate correction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These associations are more pronounced for some indicators, especially online\nand offline participation, among users with more polarized affect directed towards US\npolitical leaders.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "of the eighteen interaction terms, eight\nremain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "When a user’s online political engagement increased, higher hostility tended to follow on all three measures. The reverse sequence also appeared, but less consistently.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement\nare followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures. We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this\npattern is weaker and less consistent.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "More hostile users posted more, had more followers, and followed somewhat more accounts. Comparing average hostility with a level 1.5 standard deviations higher, estimated posts rose from about 420 to 900 and followers from about 220 to 370.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers\nof followers and, to a lesser extent, friends.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above\nthe mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the\nnumber of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The main study concerns US users of a public, highly political platform in a deliberately selected polarized setting. It does not establish that the associations transfer to other countries, platforms, or private conversations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data collection focused on the United States specifically because the associations of interest may be especially\nvisible in highly polarized political environments.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "This platform is a public, textbased, and highly political platform, and the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances, audiences, moderation systems, or norms of interaction.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Cross-platform research is needed to assess\nwhether similar patterns appear on Facebook, Reddit, YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, or private messaging environments.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcome is hostility grounded in political differences. It neither encompasses every toxic exchange nor singles out the narrower category of hate speech.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, toxic online exchanges in the aftermath of, say, a\nsports game fall outside the purview of what we examine here.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "We prefer to use the term “political hostility” rather\nthan “political hate” since we do not wish to confuse this more general measure of online political hostility with the narrower concept of “hate speech” which we are not purporting to measure.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Even the validated text score can mistake vocabulary about political conflict for an expression of hostility. The authors interpret it as an aggregate indicator, not a definitive judgment about each post.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Because the measure is based on proximity\nto an embedding space built from political and hostile terms, it may partly capture proximity to politically salient conflict\nlanguage or hate-related political vocabulary rather than hostility alone.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We therefore interpret the SU score as an\naggregate indicator of online political hostility and assess the robustness of the findings using additional measures.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The observational results do not establish that engagement causes hostility. Within-person models account for stable differences but leave open time-varying confounding, measurement error, and influence in both directions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The RI-CLPM analyses reduce concerns about stable time-invariant confounding\nby focusing on within-person change, but they do not rule out time-varying confounding, reciprocal relationships, or measurement error. Political sophistication or partisan involvement may be common causes of both participation and hostility.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not establish the causal ordering of the engagement indicators or an effect of education on hostility independent of engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Third, and relatedly, the present findings are mute on the exact causal relationships between the different indicators of political engagement and their antecedents.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "An important caveat in this respect is that the analyses should not be taken to imply\nthat, for example, more educated users are more hostile absent their generally higher levels of political engagement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization measure concerns party leaders, not parties as institutions or their ordinary supporters; the study cannot say whether those other measures would yield the same conditional pattern.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fifth, due to data availability, our measure of affective polarization captures feelings toward party elites rather than\ntoward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters, as is standard [22].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Future research should examine whether the same\nconditional patterns appear using party-directed or supporter-directed measures.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction estimates do not show that polarization itself turns engagement into hostility. Polarization could precede hostile interactions, accompany them, or follow them.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This distinction is especially important because leader-directed affective\npolarization may be an antecedent, correlate, or consequence of hostile online interaction. We therefore interpret these\nanalyses as suggestive evidence of conditional associations, not as causally identified moderation effects.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not decide between grievance-based and personality-based explanations. In particular, adjustment for the Big Five does not directly test explanations based on sadism or Dark Triad traits, which the survey did not measure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures. Instead, it asks whether online political hostility is also associated with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "These controls are not intended as a direct test of Dark Triad,\nsadism, or other antagonistic-trait accounts of online hostility. The survey did not include measures of those more specific\ntraits, and we therefore cannot adjudicate those accounts directly.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Posting and connection counts indicate potential reach only. Neither those counts nor the descriptive network graphs establish influence, centrality, or community structure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These results suggest greater activity and potential reach, but they\nshould not be interpreted as evidence of formal network influence or centrality.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "For completeness, we include exploratory descriptive\nnetwork visualizations of the study participants’ follower/friend graph in S1 Appendix but do not draw inferences about\ncentrality or community structure.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors retain the classical civic-engagement distinction between capacities, motivation, and participation. They use it to separate antecedents, indicators, and behaviors rather than combine them into a single political-resource scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Third, online and offline political participation are\nbehavioral indicators of engagement. This distinction follows the theoretical framework above and avoids treating all\nvariables as interchangeable indicators of a single resource construct.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "While some studies investigate the relationships between these different types of indicators and measures [18,19], we will\nin the present study treat them as separate indicators as they may not operate through the same pathways.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They extend the revisionist literature on the costs of engagement, including motivated reasoning and partisan animosity, from biased political judgments and misinformation sharing to hostile expression on social media.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This pattern is consistent with recent research on motivated reasoning, partisan animus, and the darker sides of political engagement [21,33,47,82].",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Importantly, prior research has only to a limited extent applied this revisionist perspective om\npolitical engagement to politics on social media and mainly in the context of misinformation sharing [53]. Here, we extend\nand integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on\nonline political hostility.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They add engagement to existing accounts of online hostility rather than rejecting socioeconomic grievance or antagonistic personality as explanations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We extend this revisionist\nperspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression\non X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They combine engagement theory with research on affective polarization to propose a conditional argument: engagement supplies opportunities to participate, while partisan feelings may bear on whether that participation is antagonistic.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Engagement provides attention, motivation, skills, and opportunities to participate in political discussion. Affective polarization may shape whether such participation is expressed in\nantagonistic terms toward political opponents.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We therefore interpret the moderation analyses as descriptive\nevidence about conditional associations rather than as evidence that affective polarization causally moderates the effect\nof engagement on hostility.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Almond and Verba argue that democratic citizenship requires restraint as well as activity. The authors build on that qualification to the classical praise of engagement by asking whether active citizens can also make political exchanges more hostile.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In particular [20], argued that engagement needed to be tempered with a more parochial relationship to politics such that “the democratic citizen is called on to pursue contradictory\ngoals: he must be active, yet passive; involved, yet not too involved; influential, yet deferential.”",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, political engagement and its\nantecedents may be associated with rather than diminish online political hostility.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Almond GA, Verba S. The civic culture: Political attitudes and democracy in five nations.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
